17] [17592186162732 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186161915 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186162733 #edb/ref 1061 135399 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186162733 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "a9b790a0-3cdf-5858-9145-025944236be0" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186162733 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186161916 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186163366 #edb/ref 1064 "We can use fraction models to multiply any two fractions. \n\nLet's figure out how to use models to compute the following product:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{3}{5} \\times \\dfrac{2}{3}\n$$\n\nWe start with the model of $\\dfrac{3}{5}.$\n\n![](images/06/06869533fb9eae08e88187c0965c9683c3b00e8cf269b121f416ce7f473ad508.png)\n\nSince we need to multiply by $\\dfrac{\\color{red}2}{\\color{blue}3}$, we split the whole into $\\color{blue}3$ equal parts *horizontally*. But this time, we keep the shaded pieces in the bottom $\\color{red}2$ rows.\n\n![](images/0c/0c449cdff6dabfd6599925b0d450ab2d579ce49d2729b9a98fe63bb8992a850d.png)\n\nFrom the picture on the right, we see that the shaded part represents $\\dfrac{6}{15}$ of the whole.\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{3}{5} \\times \\dfrac{2}{3} = \\dfrac{6}{15}.\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186163366 #edb/ref 1065 8978 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186163366 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "98bc8936-f81c-50ef-a2f7-b9cb50262591" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186163366 #edb/ref 1067 "Introduction" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186427397 #edb/ref 1080 "\\frac{12}{15}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186427397 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186427397 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "01d63f8c-c803-595e-b693-12e39e04dd26" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186427398 #edb/ref 1080 "images/db/db6ff5f88c9c2aa708a6cfd06fb886ef20ee28297d0b902881abbaae816e8910.png" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186427398 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045497 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186427398 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "0255e2aa-48dc-5811-9f11-82938ddaf491" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186427399 #edb/